# EMBERDEEP — Act III: The Mycelium Hollows (floors 9-12). Party: Wren, Brannoc, Maelis; Pip joins on floor 10.

=== floor_9_enter
!music Hollows
@narrator: The Mycelium Hollows. The stair ends in a cavern so vast its roof is lost in a haze of drifting spores, gold and blue and violet, turning like dust in a sunbeam.
@narrator: Mushrooms stand tall as chapel towers. Moss brightens under each footstep and dims when the foot lifts. Everything glows, and everything is fading at the edges, like a painting left out in the rain.
@maelis: Mycelial luminescence. The Order's naturalists catalogued over forty — sorry. You don't want footnotes.
@wren: I want you where I can see you.
@narrator: They walk on without speaking. It is a long, glowing, terrible silence.
@brannoc: By the deep roots. The pair of you.

=== floor_10_enter
@narrator: Figures move between the mushroom stalks: slow, grey, heads bowed. They wear Hollowmere clothes. A baker's apron. A miner's belt.
@wren: Those are people. That's Garrick, from the mill. He went missing at midwinter.
@maelis: Hollow. The Pale takes the colour first, then the names. Then they drift downward, toward it.
@wren: Garrick! It's Wren! Wren Varn, the lamplighter!
@narrator: The grey man turns his face toward her voice, the way a plant turns toward a window. Then he turns away and keeps walking down.
@brannoc: Can't help him, lass. Not yet.
@narrator: Somewhere ahead, among the stalks, someone small is shouting. It is the angriest small voice Wren has ever heard.

=== pip_meet
@narrator: In a ring of dying glowroots, a creature no taller than Wren's knee, with mossy hair, bark-brown skin and eyes like drops of dew, is swinging a twig at a crowd of Hollow Ones.
@pip: Go AWAY! Shoo! These roots are SLEEPING! You are standing on somebody's grandmother!
!sfx Light
@narrator: Wren raises Old Tom high. The Hollow Ones flinch from the light, and one by one they turn and drift back into the dark.
@pip: Oh! You have a sun in a box!
@wren: It's a lantern. His name is Old Tom.
@pip: Hello, Tom-lantern. Thank you for the shining.
@wren: Tom says you're very welcome.
@brannoc: Here we go.
@pip: I'm Pip Thistledown, last tender of the Glowroot Grove. The grove is very old and very kind and very poorly. The grey is in her roots.
@pip: Everyone else went to sleep. I'm not sleepy. I'm the tender. Tenders don't sleep till the tending's done.
@maelis: A spriggan. I was told they were extinct. Footnote: I am delighted to be wrong.
@pip: You're going down where the cold comes from. I can smell it on your boots.
@wren: We're going to find my sister. And stop the cold, if we can.
@pip: Then I'm coming! The Mother is at the heart of the grove, and she's sick, and she won't answer me. And if I find her Everbloom seed, the grove can start again.
@brannoc: It's knee-high.
@pip: I'm knee-high to YOU, Big-beard. To the moss I'm enormous.
!join pip
!quest start everbloom
!sfx LevelUp

=== floor_11_enter
@pip: Lantern-friend. Why does Book-lady always walk at the back?
@wren: It's complicated, Pip.
@pip: The moss says complicated means "I am sad and cross at the same time".
@maelis: The moss is annoyingly perceptive.
@pip: Big-beard, will you carry me over the puddle, please?
@brannoc: Walk.
@narrator: Brannoc sighs, lifts Pip onto his shoulder, and does not put him down for the rest of the floor.

=== floor_12_enter
@narrator: The glowroots thicken here, roots as broad as bridges, every one of them leading inward. At the heart of the Hollows something vast breathes long, slow, grey breaths.
@pip: That's her. That's the Mother. She used to sing every morning. Now she only breathes.
@pip: Maybe she's only tired. Maybe she'll know me.
@maelis: Pip —
@pip: I know, Book-lady. I'm saying maybe so my feet keep walking.
@wren: Maybe is a good word. We'll hold on to it together.

=== mother_pre
!music Boss
@narrator: The heart of the grove is a great hollow tree of root and cap. Its canopy is hung with living lights like lanterns, and half of them have gone grey.
@narrator: From the roots rises a tall, soft, many-armed shape crowned with failing glow. Her face is made of folded gills and moss.
@mother: Little tender. You came home.
@pip: Mother! It's Pip! I kept the east roots. I sang to the new caps every morning, the way you taught me.
@mother: So cold, little tender. So cold, so tired. Come down into the roots with me. Come under. We will sleep, all of us, and never be cold again.
@pip: That's not you. You never said "never". You always said "not yet".
@mother: Come... under...
@narrator: Grey spores pour from her crown like snow.
@wren: Pip, get behind me.
@pip: No, Lantern-friend. She's my Mother. I stand in front.
!sfx BossRoar
!shake
!battle mother_of_spores

=== mother_post
!music Hollows
@narrator: The grey drains from the Mother's crown. For a moment the old gold returns to her lights, bright and brief, like the last warm day of autumn.
@mother: Pip. Little tender. You stood in front.
@pip: I did. You taught me.
@mother: The grove is finished, little one. Grieve it. Then go and grow.
@narrator: Something falls from the heart of the tree into Pip's hands: a seed, warm and gold, that beats faintly against his palm.
!give everbloom_seed
!sfx ItemGet
@pip: Goodbye, Mother. I'll tend it. I'll find it a place with light.
@narrator: The great tree sighs and settles. Its last lanterns go out one by one, gently, like candles blown out at the end of a long evening.
@narrator: Pip does not cry. Spriggans can't, Maelis will explain later, in a footnote. Instead he presses his face into the moss and stays there a long time.
@narrator: Nobody hurries him. Brannoc takes off his hat. Wren sets Old Tom down beside Pip, so he won't be in the dark.
@maelis: We should say something. People say something, at these.
@wren: She stood between the grey and her grove for as long as she could.
@brannoc: And her tender stood in front.
@pip: Big-beard. May I ride on your shoulder for a while?
@brannoc: Long as you like.
